# Native DwarfSpec Command Migration

## Goal

Replace custom native-screen test harnesses and direct interaction shortcuts
with DwarfSpec's borrowed native-screen APIs. Native interaction proofs must
exercise the real DF screen and DFHack overlay registry without creating a
test-owned screen above them.

## Command conventions

- Use `ds.mountNativeScreen()` for tests that interact with the live DF UI.
- Use `ds.get(path)` for native controls that are exposed as widgets; native is
  the default source.
- Use `ds.get(path, {source='overlay', overlay=name})` for controls in a
  registered overlay; the installed DwarfSpec declaration requires the
  explicit overlay source.
- Do not use `ds.root()` for UI selection.
- Use `ds.move_pointer(x, y)` for exact native screen cells.
- Use subject `input()`, `click()`, `hover()`, `redraw()`, `inspect()`,
  `text()`, and `getFocusList()` for interaction and observation.
- Use `ds.capture_view_tree()` to record unresolved native or overlay widget
  paths before introducing test-only discovery code. Procedural game UI, such
  as the Hauling route rows, is outside that widget tree.
- Use `ds.capture_screen()` when a bounded screen snapshot is stronger than
  a sequence of individual tile reads.
- Keep direct raw DF data reads only for domain-state assertions that have no
  widget equivalent, such as `plotinfo.hauling` route/scroll state, route stop
  coordinates, or map viewport state.
- Treat the base-game Hauling route list as procedural screen-cell UI, not as
  an addressable native widget tree. Exercise it through the borrowed native
  screen's input path, derive its screen cells through the production layout,
  and use rendered output plus `plotinfo.hauling` state for observation.
- Use `plotinfo.hauling` only for domain-state assertions. It is not a widget
  root and must not be passed to `ds.get()`.

## Phase 0: Establish the native command baseline

- [x] Make this document the authoritative execution checklist for the native
      DwarfSpec migration.
- [x] Mark the earlier mounted-host routing and custom-screen minecart work in
      `natural-widget-interaction-tests.todo` as historical and superseded.
- [x] Confirm the installed DwarfSpec package exposes `mountNativeScreen()`,
      exact-coordinate `move_pointer(x, y)`, native and registered-overlay
      control lookup through `get()`, subject inspection, focus, text, and
      screen/view-tree capture commands.
- [x] Record the minimum DwarfSpec version or explicit capability check required
      by DwarfUI's native tests.
- [x] Capture the native Hauling screen and record the calculated route-list
      bounds and row geometry used by the minecart test. Document that the
      route and stop rows are procedurally rendered from
      `plotinfo.hauling.view_routes`, `view_stops`, and `scroll_position` and
      therefore have no stable native widget path for `ds.get(...)`.
- [x] Record the `plotinfo.hauling` fields used strictly as state evidence
      beside the native interaction test.
- [x] Capture the native fortress view tree and record the stable top-bar
      moodlet controls or the evidence that their hit regions are screen-cell
      based rather than native widget controls. The baseline uses
      `TopBarMoodDisplay:find_layout()` to verify all seven production
      screen-cell regions; no native widget path is required for moodlets.
- [x] Capture the registered minecart and mood overlay trees and record their
      stable control paths.
- [x] Capture the staged tooltip overlay tree and record its stable target
      path after registration.
- [x] Document every resolved control path and every procedural screen-cell
      region beside the test that uses it.

## Phase 1: Migrate the minecart registered-overlay test

- [x] Start the test with `ds.mountNativeScreen()` and remove
      `MinecartNativeInteractionScreen`.
- [x] Remove the test-owned pointer `Panel`, its layout mutation, and the
      custom pointer helper.
- [x] Replace all native pointer placement with `ds.move_pointer(x, y)`.
- [x] Open and close Hauling through `ds.input()` while the borrowed native
      screen is mounted.
- [x] Confirm the mounted native screen reports the expected Hauling focus
      string before interacting with its procedural rows.
- [x] Calculate and cache the native Hauling panel bounds and visible route/stop
      row geometry from the production layout and `plotinfo.hauling` state.
- [x] Use `ds.capture_screen()` over the cached Hauling panel bounds to verify
      rendered row text and selection indicators; do not model the procedural
      rows as `ds.get(...)` subjects.
- [x] Use `plotinfo.hauling.view_routes`, `view_stops`, and `scroll_position`
      only to identify which logical row occupies a production-derived screen
      cell and to observe the result of naturally delivered input.
- [x] Select the real rail and zoom controls with
      `ds.get(path, {source='overlay', overlay=registered_name})`.
- [x] Remove direct overlay-registry table lookup where a selected overlay
      control provides the necessary rendered or inspected evidence.
- [x] Replace overlay-root state assertions with rendered selection indicators,
      control inspection, and native route/map state.
- [x] Remove the test-driven native-viewscreen lookup from production
      `get_focus()` and restore its production focus semantics.
- [x] Keep the map-paint spy only if `capture_screen()` cannot observe the
      Premium transparent marker; document why the final paint boundary is
      required.
- [x] Move to a rendered route-label cell, send normal mouse input, and assert
      that it selects the corresponding route.
- [x] Move to a rendered stop-row cell, send normal mouse input, and assert
      that it selects the corresponding route.
- [x] Assert that the selected-row indicator is rendered at the exact
      menu-relative position.
- [x] Hover a stop row and assert that the action rail is rendered at the exact
      row-relative position.
- [x] Move the pointer from the stop row onto the rail and assert that the rail
      remains open.
- [x] Click the rendered zoom action and assert that the viewport centers on the
      stop while its route remains selected.
- [x] Scroll over the rail and assert that the rail consumes the wheel without
      changing `plotinfo.hauling.scroll_position`.
- [x] Scroll with the pointer over the rendered native route-list bounds and
      assert that `plotinfo.hauling.scroll_position` changes and the captured
      visible rows change consistently.
- [x] Assert that native scrolling rebinds the rail to the newly visible stop.
- [x] Assert that the formerly bound stop is no longer eligible for activation.
- [x] Assert marker glyph, color, transparency, and label rendering for the
      selected route.
- [x] Move the native viewport and assert that marker and label positions follow
      the production world-to-UI transform.
- [x] Confirm cleanup releases the native attachment without dismissing or
      mutating the pre-existing game screen.

## Phase 2: Migrate the native mood-popover test

- [ ] Start the native top-bar scenario with `ds.mountNativeScreen()`.
- [ ] Obtain registered popover controls through
      `ds.get(path, {source='overlay',
      overlay='dwarfui-mood-popover.mood_popover'})`.
- [ ] Remove direct writes to `gps.mouse_*` and `gps.precise_mouse_*`.
- [ ] Move the pointer to every complete moodlet column with
      `ds.move_pointer(x, y)` and request the normal render path with
      `redraw()`.
- [ ] Exercise the number, icon, area above the icon, and area below the icon in
      each of the seven moodlet columns.
- [ ] Assert that every moodlet column selects its expected stress category.
- [ ] Remove direct `update_popover()` calls from the native scenario.
- [ ] Use `inspect()` for popover/list bounds and scroll position and `text()`
      for the selected category, count, and visible unit rows.
- [ ] Replace direct `gui.simulateInput()` wheel and click delivery with
      `ds.mouseInput()` at the current native pointer position.
- [ ] Remove direct `refresh()` calls from the native scenario; establish data
      then allow the registered overlay's normal update/render cadence.
- [ ] Move the pointer from a moodlet into the popover and assert that the
      popover remains open.
- [ ] Scroll while the pointer remains over a moodlet and assert that the list
      advances exactly once.
- [ ] Scroll while the pointer is over the popover list and assert that the
      scrollbar position and visible rows update consistently.
- [ ] Click a rendered unit row and assert that the game zooms to that unit.
- [ ] Assert that the unit card opens on its overview tab.
- [ ] Assert that the overview tab contains populated overview data.
- [ ] Verify native focus loss through `getFocusList()` and the registered
      overlay's actual lifecycle.
- [ ] Restore unit-card, viewport, pointer-sensitive, and overlay state after
      the native attachment is released.

## Phase 3: Reclassify deterministic mood component contracts

- [ ] Split provider-injected registered-overlay setup from the native
      production-proof scenario.
- [ ] Retain provider-injected snapshot tests for stress-category and sorting
      behavior.
- [ ] Retain deterministic layout tests that make no native interaction claim.
- [ ] Rename provider-driven suites and cases to identify component-logic
      coverage explicitly.
- [ ] Move pure `update_popover()` and `onInput()` return-value contracts into
      `.spec.lua` files when no mounted rendering or routing is asserted.
- [ ] For retained mounted component routing tests, use DwarfSpec mouse input
      and redraw commands instead of direct `onInput()` calls.
- [ ] Retain `ds.mount(MoodPopoverOverlay, ...)` only for explicitly named
      injected-provider component coverage.
- [ ] Remove descriptions that imply DFHack-native behavior when the test only
      calls a method or uses an injected provider.
- [ ] Keep injected providers out of the native moodlet production-proof
      scenario.

## Phase 4: Migrate tooltip registration integration

- [ ] Replace `TooltipRegistrationScreen` and its pointer target with
      `ds.mountNativeScreen()` and exact-cell pointer movement.
- [ ] Keep `ds.stage_overlay_registration()` for the staged registration
      source; do not substitute a fake overlay lifecycle.
- [ ] Resolve the staged overlay target through
      `ds.get(path, {source='overlay', overlay=registered_name})` after
      registration.
- [ ] Replace manual native-focus matching with `getFocusList()` and native
      subject inspection.
- [ ] Use redraw and overlay-control inspection to prove registration,
      enablement, tooltip selection, and focus ineligibility.
- [ ] Preserve cleanup of the staged registration and tooltip singleton.

## Phase 5: Normalize mounted components and audit the remaining suite

- [ ] Keep test-owned `ds.mount(...)` screens and overlays where the fixture
      itself is the subject of the test.
- [ ] Replace render-dependent `wait_frames()` calls with `redraw()` where a
      completed render is required.
- [ ] Replace any direct fixture input invocation with subject `input()` or
      DwarfSpec mouse input when validating event routing.
- [ ] Keep direct fixture screen show/dismiss calls only where the test is
      explicitly proving modal lifecycle behavior.
- [ ] Confirm neither tooltip component test claims native DF UI coverage.
- [ ] Review every remaining `.ds.lua` file for direct pointer-global mutation.
- [ ] Review every remaining `.ds.lua` file for direct production `onInput()`
      calls.
- [ ] Review every remaining `.ds.lua` file for direct render, update, or
      refresh calls that manufacture user-visible state.
- [ ] Review every remaining `.ds.lua` file for direct button activation in
      place of a rendered click.
- [ ] Review registered-overlay tests for focus, pointer, enablement, or
      lifecycle stubs.
- [ ] Add comments to each component test describing setup intent, interaction
      intent, and the state or rendering proved by its assertions.
- [ ] Add a test-review convention that rejects native-interaction claims based
      only on internal callback invocation.
- [ ] Classify and justify every remaining direct lifecycle call as a pure
      method contract, deterministic component fixture, or cleanup operation.

